---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'How do I convert CGPA to percentage?', a: 'Multiply your CGPA by the multiplier your board or university uses. For example, 8.2 × 9.5 = 77.9%. If your institution uses a different factor, type it in.' },
  { q: 'Which multiplier should I use?', a: 'Use the one printed by your own board, university or the exam notification. Some use 9.5, others use 10 or a custom table, so this tool never assumes one is official.' },
  { q: 'Can I convert percentage back to CGPA?', a: 'Yes. Switch the direction to "Percentage to CGPA". The tool divides by the multiplier, or scales against the maximum CGPA you choose.' },
  { q: 'What is the proportional method?', a: 'It is CGPA ÷ maximum CGPA × 100. It suits scales such as 4 or 5 points, and is used where a plain proportion is accepted.' },
  { q: 'Is a converted percentage an official result?', a: 'No. It is only an estimate. For forms and admissions, use the conversion or certificate your institution issues.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout slug="cgpa-to-percentage-calculator" faqs={faqs}>
  {/* AUTHOR REVIEW: lead paragraph, drafted by AI. */}
  <p slot="lead">
    This CGPA to percentage calculator lets you choose the multiplier, so you can follow the rule your own board or
    university uses. It also converts percentage back to CGPA and shows the formula with your numbers.
  </p>

  <div slot="tool">
    <div class="row two">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="cg-dir">Direction</label>
        <select id="cg-dir"><option value="toPct">CGPA to percentage</option><option value="toCgpa">Percentage to CGPA</option></select>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="cg-method">Method</label>
        <select id="cg-method"><option value="mult">Multiply by a factor</option><option value="prop">Proportional to the scale maximum</option></select>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="row two">
      <div class="field"><label for="cg-val" id="cg-vlabel">Your CGPA</label><input id="cg-val" type="number" step="any" min="0" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="e.g. 8.2" /></div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="cg-k" id="cg-klabel">Multiplier (factor)</label>
        <input id="cg-k" type="number" step="any" min="0" inputmode="decimal" value="9.5" />
        <div class="chips" id="cg-chips">
          <button type="button" class="chip" data-k="9.5" aria-pressed="true">9.5</button>
          <button type="button" class="chip" data-k="10" aria-pressed="false">10</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="result" aria-live="polite">
      <p class="label" style="margin:0">Answer</p>
      <p class="big" id="cg-out">–</p>
      <p id="cg-work" class="hint">Enter your value to see the answer and the working.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="hint">The preset multipliers are common examples, not an official rule for any institution. Confirm yours before using the result.</p>
  </div>

  {/* AUTHOR REVIEW: how-to and worked example drafted by AI. Add the conversion rules you actually see at your school or college. */}
  <h2>How to convert CGPA to percentage</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Keep "CGPA to percentage" and "Multiply by a factor" selected, unless your institution uses a proportional rule.</li>
    <li>Enter your CGPA. Then set the multiplier from your university's rule or the form instructions.</li>
    <li>Read the percentage and the working line, and copy the figure into your form if the rule allows it.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p class="worked">
    A student has a CGPA of 8.2 and the college states a multiplier of 9.5. The working is 8.2 × 9.5 = 77.9, so the
    percentage is 77.9%. With the proportional method on a 10-point scale it would be 8.2 ÷ 10 × 100 = 82%. Same CGPA,
    different answers, which is why you must use the method your institution names.
  </p>
  <h2>Why different institutions give different answers</h2>
  <p>
    CGPA is built from grade points averaged over credits, and each university decides how to translate that average
    into a percentage. Some publish a single factor, some give a table, and some do not convert at all. This tool
    is a calculator, not an authority. If a form needs an official equivalent, ask your institution for a certificate.
  </p>
  <script>import '../../scripts/cgpa.js';</script>
</ToolLayout>
